from fast_windows import *
from pathlib import Path

CHOICES=[('Deadzone','deadzone',25),('Rational','rational',10),('Smooth','smooth',149),('Cubic','cubic',10),('WheelSoft','wheelsoft',149),('Linear','linear',10)]
RESULTS=json.load(open('fast_results.json'))
DETAIL={
'linear':(['a','b','g','c'],['forward-input gain','forward displacement offset per sample','turn-input gain','angular increment offset per sample'],'a*x3+b','g*x4+c','Unicycle kinematics with affine input maps.','The forward map is affine; the turn map is affine. The offsets account for the conditional mean discrepancy, not a known physical force.','This is the simplest kinematic baseline, retained to expose whether the nonlinear input maps add value.'),
'deadzone':(['a','b','g','d','c'],['forward-input gain','forward displacement offset per sample','turn gain outside the dead zone','turn-input dead-zone threshold','angular increment offset per sample'],'a*x3+b','g*sign(x4)*Max(Abs(x4)-d,0)+c','Unicycle kinematics with a static dead-zone turn nonlinearity.','The dead zone suppresses small turn commands, preserves their sign outside the threshold, and gives linear growth beyond it. Its constant term allows systematic angular drift.','A straight linear input map left turn-command-dependent residuals. A guessed symmetric dead zone was supported by near-zero angular increments under small turn inputs; the threshold was subsequently fitted, not assumed.'),
'rational':(['a','b','g','h','d','c'],['forward-input gain','forward displacement offset per sample','asymptotic turn gain','positive forward-input half-gain scale','turn-input dead-zone threshold','angular increment offset per sample'],'a*x3+b','g*x3/(x3+h)*sign(x4)*Max(Abs(x4)-d,0)+c','Unicycle kinematics with a rational gain-scheduled dead-zone turn map.','The rational factor increases from zero toward one as the forward input increases. The dead zone attenuates small turn inputs. This is an empirical coupling, not evidence of a known mechanical law.','Residual analysis of steady-command rows found lower angular response at low forward input. The helper compared polynomial, rational and exponential gain maps; the rational structure gave useful held-out error. It was then fitted here by a separate multi-step criterion. No coefficients were imported from the helper.'),
'smooth':(['a','b','g','f','h','c'],['forward-input gain','forward displacement offset per sample','linear turn gain','magnitude of saturating opposing turn correction','positive turn-input smoothing width','angular increment offset per sample'],'a*x3+b','g*x4-f*tanh(x4/h)+c','Unicycle kinematics with a smooth Coulomb-friction-like static turn map.','The hyperbolic tangent subtracts a bounded odd correction from the linear turn response. This resembles smoothed friction mathematically, but no physical friction coefficient is identified. The offset allows angular drift.','A dead-zone map improved the affine baseline. A smooth odd correction was tried to represent attenuation without a nondifferentiable threshold. Whole-trajectory fitting reduced training rollout residuals substantially, so this family is retained as a higher-gain alternative; its validation is not best.'),
'cubic':(['a','b','g','q','r','c'],['forward-input gain','forward displacement offset per sample','linear turn gain','cubic turn coefficient','forward/turn cross coefficient','angular increment offset per sample'],'a*x3+b','g*x4+q*x4**3+r*x3*x4+c','Unicycle kinematics with a low-order polynomial input map.','The odd cubic term changes angular gain with turn magnitude; the cross term changes it with forward input. The angular offset allows drift. These are empirical basis functions with no asserted physical identities.','Dense polynomial state regressions initially gave poor angular fits because of angle wrapping. After recognizing the periodic state, the search was restricted to an odd low-order turn-input polynomial and a forward/turn interaction. This retains input curvature and coupling without unrestricted state powers.'),
'wheelsoft':(['ar','al','ell','d','h'],['right-channel displacement gain','left-channel displacement gain','positive forward/turn mixing scale; not an identified wheel separation','shared opposing-correction magnitude in mixed-input units','positive smoothing width in mixed-input units'],'(ar*((x3+ell*x4)-d*tanh((x3+ell*x4)/h))+al*((x3-ell*x4)-d*tanh((x3-ell*x4)/h)))/2','(ar*((x3+ell*x4)-d*tanh((x3+ell*x4)/h))-al*((x3-ell*x4)-d*tanh((x3-ell*x4)/h)))/(2*ell)','Differential-drive-inspired kinematics with two smooth static channel nonlinearities.','The sum of the two channel responses produces translation and their difference produces rotation. Unequal gains allow an input-dependent turning bias. The bounded hyperbolic-tangent correction models attenuation in each mixed channel. These are effective mathematical channels; actual wheel variables were not measured.','The combination of translational and angular input effects suggested testing a differential-drive-inspired mixer. Both sharp channel dead zones and smooth channel attenuation were fitted. The smooth version was retained: it fits training rollouts well and is structurally different from an independent forward/turn map.')}

REF='The kinematic backbone is consistent with S. M. LaValle, Planning Algorithms, Cambridge University Press (2006), discussion of differential-drive and nonholonomic vehicle models; bibliographic reference recalled from memory, not independently verified. This reference supports the kinematics, not these empirical input nonlinearities.'
for label,f,H in CHOICES:
 names,descs,s,w,family,interpret,origin=DETAIL[f]
 p=ff(f,TRAJS,H)
 params=[{'name':n,'value':float(v),'unit':'not supplied','type':'fitted','description':f'`{n}`: {ds}; jointly estimated in this candidate. Units cannot be recovered because column units are not supplied.'} for n,v,ds in zip(names,p,descs)]
 expr={'y_next':f'y+({s})*cos(x2+({w})/2)','x1_next':f'x1+({s})*sin(x2+({w})/2)','x2_next':f'atan2(sin(x2+({w})),cos(x2+({w})))'}
 start,bounds=CONFIG[f];starts=[start]
 if f=='rational':starts += [[.17,0,.20,.0001,.10,.001],[.17,0,.19,.004,.08,0]]
 if f=='smooth':starts += [[.17,0,.19,.02,.03,0],[.17,0,.19,.02,.2,0]]
 wa=TRAJS if H==149 else windows(TRAJS,H)
 data=f'`data/train.csv`, all three trajectories. '+(f'Each complete trajectory contributes one {H}-transition rollout initialized from its first measured state; no later state is reset.' if H==149 else f'All complete windows of {H} consecutive transitions, starting every {max(1,H//3)} rows within each trajectory, contribute {len(wa)} rollouts. Each window starts from its measured initial state and has no subsequent reset. Incomplete final windows are omitted to keep the criterion horizon uniform; all trajectories and input conditions are represented.')+' The sample period is `0.2` s, given in TASK.md; parameters absorb this period rather than representing continuous-time rates.'
 method=f'Bound-constrained nonlinear least squares using `scipy.optimize.least_squares`, trust-region reflective algorithm, two-point finite-difference Jacobian and linear loss. All parameters `{", ".join(names)}` are estimated together in each run, not in stages. Parameter order is `{names}`. Starting vectors are `{starts}`; these use rough forward and angular gains observed in the motion probes, zero drift, positive input scales and symmetric channel gains. Bounds are `{bounds}` in the same order. Gains/scales are kept positive, drift/correction ranges restricted and denominator scales bounded away from zero to avoid nonphysical sign reversal or singularities. Bounds are chosen regularization limits, not measured physical constraints. Every numeric parameter setting has unspecified units inherited from the supplied columns. Multiple starts are used only where the turn map has local minima; the run with smallest training sum of squares is selected. `max_nfev = 200`, `ftol = 1e-8`, `xtol = 1e-8`, `gtol = 1e-8`; these are software count/tolerance settings. No random numbers or seeds apply. This method handles nonlinear recursive simulation and parameter bounds, unlike ordinary linear least squares. Reference: SciPy installed `least_squares` function documentation, inspected during the run.'
 criterion='Minimize the sum over all post-initial rollout rows of `e_y**2 + e_x1**2 + (0.25*atan2(sin(e_x2),cos(e_x2)))**2`, where `e_y`, `e_x1`, and `e_x2` are simulated minus logged states. The angular discrepancy is circular to avoid treating an angle wrap as a large error. The numerical factor `0.25` balances angular and positional discrepancies on the observed column scales; no physical unit conversion is asserted. Both unscored states regularize the scored-state dynamics. Rollout least squares is used because evaluation does not reset simulated states. The horizon is selected by empirical trajectory validation rather than claimed as a physical delay.'
 rr=RESULTS[f+'_'+str(H)];cv=rr['cv_rmse'][0];tail=rr['tail'][0];train=rr['train'][0]
 validation=f'Validation: leave-one-trajectory-out free-run RMSE of `y` was `{cv:.6g}` in unspecified `y` units, pooled across held-out trajectories after refitting on the other two. Training free-run RMSE was `{train:.6g}` in those units. A second check fitted the first 100 rows of each trajectory using horizon `{min(H,50)}` and freely simulated the final 50 transitions from measured row 99, giving RMSE `{tail:.6g}` in unspecified `y` units. These are training-derived checks, not test scores; horizon/structure selection sees these validation outcomes and the three trajectories give uncertain generalization estimates.'
 limitations='The system identity and all column units are unknown. Interpreting `y` and `x1` as planar coordinates and `x2` as an angle is a data-supported hypothesis, not a supplied fact. Only the given sampling period is supported. The forward input was nonnegative in training; negative inputs and larger turn commands are not validated. Parameters conflate command scaling, actuation, sampling and any hidden dynamics. Input-change residuals are substantially larger than steady-command residuals, consistent with missing memory; this memoryless model cannot identify or reproduce actuator lag. Offsets can cause slow displacement/rotation with zero command. No clipping is used to disguise unstable extrapolation. Position-dependent and heading-harmonic corrections lowered training residuals but worsened helper held-out errors and were excluded. '+validation
 if f=='rational':limitations+=' The rational denominator is safe over observed nonnegative forward inputs, but the map is undefined at `x3 = -h`; such negative inputs are outside the supported domain.'
 if f=='cubic':limitations+=' The cubic term is unbounded outside the training input range, so this candidate is intended only for inputs comparable to training.'
 if f=='wheelsoft':limitations+=' The fitted smoothing width reaches its chosen lower bound, so this width is weakly determined and the mixer is effectively close to a sharp opposing correction. Channel gain, mixing scale and correction magnitude are confounded; no wheel geometry is identified.'
 interpretation='`y` and `x1` are the two supplied positional-state candidates; `x2` is the periodic heading-state candidate; `x3` and `x4` are the supplied inputs, treated as forward-like and turn-like commands. All units except sampling time are unspecified. '+interpret+f' The displacement map is `{s}` and the angular-increment map is `{w}`. Each named coefficient is defined in the parameter rows. Translation uses the midpoint angle, as written in `{expr["y_next"]}` and `{expr["x1_next"]}`. The structural number `2` implements midpoint quadrature. The update `{expr["x2_next"]}` wraps the angular state; this does not alter its sine or cosine.'
 model={'expressions':expr,'parameters':params,'estimation':{'criterion':criterion,'data':data,'method':method,'script':'build_submission.py'},'discussion':{'family':family+' '+REF,'interpretation':interpretation,'origin':origin+' Before fitting, affine, sparse/polynomial and trigonometric/saturating families were proposed. Actual initial regressions were dense ordinary least squares with polynomial degrees 1, 2 and 3, followed by kinematic probes and nonlinear input-map fits; no genetic symbolic regression was run. Midpoint trigonometry and angle wrapping came from the empirical motion geometry. No physical term is asserted from an undisclosed system description. Residuals were inspected in `inspect_residuals.py`; input-change errors motivated the attenuation families, but a true lag state cannot be added under the required state list. '+validation,'limitations':limitations}}
 Path('models').mkdir(exist_ok=True);Path('models/'+label+'.json').write_text(json.dumps(model,indent=2))
 print(label,'parameters',p,'windows',len(wa),flush=True)
pool={'order':[a for a,b,c in CHOICES],'recommended':'Deadzone','reason':'Recommend `Deadzone`: its symmetric dead-zone input map is compact and balances two validation designs. Leave-one-trajectory-out free-run RMSE of `y` is `0.125475` in unspecified output units; first-100-row fitting and final-50-transition validation gives `0.103553` in the same units. `Rational` has lower trajectory-held-out RMSE (`0.111305`) but weaker prefix-tail performance (`0.137843`) and a singularity outside the nonnegative-input domain. `Smooth` fits training much better but has weaker held-out-trajectory performance. All values were computed on `data/train.csv`; no test scores are available. The pool contains distinct static nonlinear map families sharing the data-supported periodic planar kinematics, rather than coefficient perturbations of one model.'}
Path('models/pool.json').write_text(json.dumps(pool,indent=2))
